<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A1.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="A1.T1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Model architecture summary.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We also note important tokenization differences. Qwen and Llama treat <span id="A1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">,\n</span> as a single token, while Gemma treats
<span id="A1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">,</span> and <span id="A1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">\n</span> as separate tokens.
This places the last word <math id="A1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> at position <math id="A1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math> in Qwen and Llama and position <math id="A1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-2</annotation></semantics></math> in Gemma.</p>
</div>
